## SAGE

SAGE is DXOS's AI layer: models, the skills that tell them how to work, and the agents that act in a space. An agent
works through the same operations a person invokes, reads and writes the same ECHO objects, and keeps its memory as
ECHO objects too, so what an agent did is data the user owns. Every page starts from ECHO: the objects an agent can
see and the operations it can call on them. The section has only Start here, Core concepts and Resources, like MESH,
[D-271], [D-279].

### Start here

```mdl
page sage-overview: Overview
  What SAGE is, and where it runs.
  section: SAGE
  route: /docs/sage/overview/
  group: Start here
  template: concept
  status: spike
  covers:
    - What SAGE is in one paragraph, on its own: durable agent processes with an identity of their own, typed tools, skills and any model [D-280]
    - A table of where SAGE runs, in Composer's assistant, in agents on EDGE and behind the MCP server, each linking its concept page
    - After the table, one sentence each on operations as the agent's actions and on memory as objects in the user's spaces [D-280]
    - Where to go next: Why SAGE?, then Agents
```
```mdl
page why-sage: Why SAGE?
  What sets SAGE's agents apart from other agent frameworks.
  section: SAGE
  route: /docs/sage/why-sage/
  group: Start here
  template: narrative
  status: spike
  covers:
    - The pattern: an agent handed a long task that hibernates, delegates, and resumes when its sub-agents report
    - Comparison: an agent framework with tools and a memory store, against SAGE, row by row, without claiming the others lack tools or memory [D-280]
    - Practical problems: work that outlives a session, knowing which agent changed what, waking on events without polling a model, one set of actions for people and agents, a spec that people and agents both work from and check against [D-281], choosing any model
    - Memory the user owns, as one of the practical problems
    - What SAGE does not do: it does not run the work, which EDGE's compute does, and it does not train models
```

