## PLEX

PLEX builds apps from plugins. Composer is built on it and is the app the section's examples run
in, so each page teaches the framework first and shows the result in Composer. The routes are under
`/docs/plex/`.

### Start here

```mdl
page app-framework-welcome: Overview
  What PLEX is, how an app is made of plugins, and Composer as the app built on it.
  section: PLEX
  route: /docs/plex/overview/
  group: Start here
  template: narrative
  status: spike
  covers:
    - What PLEX is, in one short intro: an app is the plugins it loads, and Composer is the app built on it, where a third-party plugin has the same powers as a built-in one [D-284]
    - What a reader can build, right after the intro: the four live demos from `/echo`, each with a narrative about the plugins that render it, then anything a built-in plugin can [F-7.10] [D-284]
    - How it works, in one section: plugins made of modules that contribute capabilities, the two packages, and what a plugin gets for free (ECHO sync, operations and skills as agent tools, TILE's look) [D-280] [D-284]
    - Where to go next: Why PLEX?, Build with a coding agent, or the learning path
```

```mdl
page why-plex: Why PLEX?
  Why an app made of plugins, where a third-party plugin has the same powers as a built-in one.
  section: PLEX
  route: /docs/plex/why-plex/
  group: Start here
  template: narrative
  status: spike
  covers:
    - The pattern: a plugin fills a role in a view it did not write, and the app renders whatever answers
    - Comparison: an app with a fixed core that outside code hooks into, against an app that is the plugins it loads
    - Practical problems: extending a plugin you did not write, loading only what is used, one action for people and agents, data that syncs without plugin code
    - What PLEX does not do: it does not store or sync data, and it is not a component library
```

```mdl
page plex-build-with-agent: Build with a coding agent
  Have Claude Code, Codex or Cursor build a plugin and load it into your Composer, from a prompt you can edit first.
  section: PLEX
  route: /docs/plex/build-with-a-coding-agent/
  group: Start here
  template: tutorial
  status: spike
  covers:
    - A prompt the reader can edit, prefilled with the World Clock example: a list of timezones for each space, as a map of pins above a row of ticking clocks [D-193]
    - A picker above the prompt that fills it with one of five examples: World clock, Sankey chart, Receipts filter, QR code canvas tool and Tournament bracket [D-184, D-191, D-192]
    - Examples written as instructions: each names the plugin and says what it should provide [D-190]
    - That only Composer Preview loads plugins from a dev server, so the reader signs in there first; the fixed lines point the agent at it
    - That the reader opens an empty folder in their agent first, since the links cannot choose or make one
    - Buttons that open the prompt in Claude Code, Codex or Cursor, and one that copies it; each puts a step the reader does not see before their text, which installs the `composer-plugin-dev` skill into the folder and has the agent follow it
    - What the reader does when the agent hands off: open Settings, then Plugins, click Enable under Dev Server, allow the browser's local-network prompt, and work through the agent's checklist
```

